A||||10||fiero56||Select cable freeze||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||01-20-2000||08:19 PM||||The select cable on my Formula has frozen on me three times today. Temps have been in the 20s. I started the car this morning and the select cable wouldn't budge. But after 5 minutes or so, it moves properly. I'm assuming there is a little water in the line near the exhaust pipe, that melts as the air around it warms. Is there a rubber boot or anything that is supposed to go around the opening near the bracket? What should I do to prevent this from happening again?<P><BR>Figure I should add the shift cable is fine. Works properly, and the rubber boot is on it, but is in a half dozen separate rings.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||loafer87gt||01-20-2000||08:54 PM||||There is a little plastic seal that goes around the end of the shift selector cables. I am going to replace mine sometime this weekend as I have the same problem. Ice gets in there and you can't shift until the heat from the manifold melts it down. Ahh, the joys of owning a fiero in cold weather.||||reg||1
Z||000002||loafer87gt||01-20-2000||08:54 PM||||There is a little plastic seal that goes around the end of the shift selector cables. I am going to replace mine sometime this weekend as I have the same problem. Ice gets in there and you can't shift until the heat from the manifold melts it down. Ahh, the joys of owning a fiero in cold weather.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||01-21-2000||01:07 PM||||My Formula has the same problem. I plan on trying the method in the link below but have not gotten around to it yet.<P> <A HREF="http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/shifter-cables.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/shifter-cables.html</A> ||||reg||1
